JetOS tests
===========

Conformance tests for the APEX services (ARINC 653 Part 1) and libpok's C
library. Each test is a JetOS project like the examples: it builds a kernel
image with one or more partitions, runs in QEMU, and checks the return
codes and behaviour of the services.

Running
-------

From the top-level directory:

    make run                     # build and run all tests (x86_64-qemu)
    make BSP=arm64-qemu run      # the same for arm64
    make run-<name>              # build and run one test, e.g. make run-mutex
    make gdb-test                # run tests/gdbserver with GDB
    make run VERBOSE=0           # show only the result of each test
    make tests                   # only build them
    make clean-tests

make run shows the output of each test while it runs (the kernel and
partition messages, indented), then its result.

The tests are built in Docker, like the examples; running them needs
qemu-system-x86_64 or qemu-system-aarch64 on the host. tests/run_test.sh
runs one built test:

    tests/run_test.sh <name> [<board>]

with TIMEOUT=<seconds> (default 60) and VERBOSE=1 to show the output
(default 0). It prints one result line per test, for example

    PASS tests/mutex on x86_64-qemu: 101 checks

or FAIL followed by the failed checks. The full QEMU output is in
tests/<name>/build/<board>/test.log. make run runs all tests and fails if
any of them failed.

Tests
-----

apex    Two partitions. P1 is started four times and checks: APEX type
        sizes; REPORT_APPLICATION_MESSAGE and RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR
        (lengths, bad addresses, LENGTH 0); CREATE_PROCESS priority,
        period and time capacity checks; partition restarts by
        SET_PARTITION_MODE and by the health monitor, and a module restart
        (start conditions PARTITION_RESTART, HM_PARTITION_RESTART,
        HM_MODULE_RESTART); CREATE_SAMPLING_PORT checks, including refresh
        periods above 2^32 ms; case-insensitive names (ports, processes,
        memory blocks); GET_MEMORY_BLOCK_STATUS; CREATE_ERROR_HANDLER and
        CONFIGURE_ERROR_HANDLER; INITIALIZE_PROCESS_CORE_AFFINITY,
        GET_MY_PROCESSOR_CORE_ID and GET_MY_INDEX; and that the
        initialization-only services return INVALID_MODE in NORMAL mode.
        P2 checks its start conditions and the validity of sampling
        messages with a huge and a tiny refresh period.

libc    One partition. Checks libpok's C library: string and memory
        functions (strncpy padding and truncation, overlapping memmove,
        strtok, strncasecmp), snprintf formats and truncation, the ctype
        classes, gmtime/asctime, and every strftime conversion, including
        ISO 8601 weeks at year boundaries.

mutex   One partition. Checks every mutex service and return code:
        CREATE_MUTEX limits and parameters, GET_MUTEX_ID, GET_MUTEX_STATUS,
        mutexes used by the main process during initialization, the
        priority ceiling (raised on acquire, restored on release, refused
        for higher-priority processes), recursive locking up to
        MAX_LOCK_LEVEL, time-outs, hand-over to waiting processes in
        PRIORITY order, SET_PRIORITY of an owner, RESET_MUTEX of a stopped
        owner, a waiting process that is stopped, PREEMPTION_LOCK_MUTEX in
        GET_PROCESS_MUTEX_STATE, and the error handler.

gdbserver
        Two partitions built from the same source, so that the same code
        and data are at the same addresses, and a time slot for the GDB
        partition. A process stores the system time in gdb_time, calls
        gdb_marker() and writes gdb_counter every 100 ms. Without GDB it
        checks that the system runs with the GDB partition in the
        schedule. make gdb-test runs it with GDB
        (tests/gdbserver/run_gdb_test.sh, smoke.gdb and smoke.py):
        connecting and attaching to both partitions, the thread list,
        breakpoints in one partition only, stepping and finish, the
        registers (integer, x87 and SSE on x86_64; integer and FP/SIMD
        on arm64), writing registers and memory, a hardware watchpoint in
        the other partition, breakpoints in both partitions, hardware
        breakpoints and watchpoints in the kernel, breakpoints hidden
        from memory reads, a stop of 3 s
        between two periods which are still about 100 ms apart in the
        system time (it doesn't run while GDB holds the system), Ctrl-C
        while the system runs and during a 'next' which doesn't end, and
        that the system goes on after GDB detaches. gdb-multiarch runs in
        the build image (or on the host with NATIVE=1); QEMU runs on the
        host.

hypervisor
        Not a JetOS project: configurations of the hypervisor
        (hypervisor/), which make hv-test builds and
        tests/hypervisor/run_hv_test.sh runs in QEMU, on x86_64 or
        with BSP=arm64-qemu on arm64: apex-mutex (tests/apex and
        tests/mutex pass side by side), bad-mem (a VM with too little
        RAM stops with a nested page fault, a stage-2 abort on arm64,
        while the other VM passes), reset (the monitor's reset command
        restarts a VM, which boots again), bad-crc (the image passes the
        CRC check at boot, and a copy with one bit flipped stops with a
        CRC mismatch before any VM starts) and gdb (run_gdb_test.sh with
        tests/gdbserver as a VM, HV_QEMU).

Some expected return codes come from public sources rather than the
standard's text (see the comments in the tests); check them against
ARINC 653 when in doubt.

Writing a test
--------------

A test is a directory tests/<name>/ with an SConstruct, a config.xml and
one directory per partition (with SConstruct, SConscript, config.xml and
src/), like an example. make finds it automatically. Its output must
follow two rules, which tests/run_test.sh relies on:

  - each check prints a line "TEST <description>: PASS" or
    "TEST <description>: FAIL ...". No other line may contain FAIL;
  - each partition prints, as its last line, "<partition> DONE: <n>
    failure(s)". The test is finished when every partition has printed it,
    and passes if every <n> is 0 and no line contains FAIL.

A partition that restarts must print its DONE line only once, in its last
phase.
